{
  "book": {
    "fb2": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<FictionBook xmlns=\"http://www.gribuser.ru/xml/fictionbook/2.0\"><description><title-info><genre>prose</genre><author><first-name>Tamsin</first-name><last-name>Vale</last-name></author><book-title>The Lighthouse Almanac</book-title><lang>en</lang></title-info></description><body><title><p>The Lighthouse Almanac</p></title><section><title><p>I. The Keeper</p></title><p>The lighthouse stood at the end of a long spit of shingle, and on clear evenings its shadow reached almost to the village. Mara had kept it for eleven years, and in that time she had learned that the sea never repeated itself.</p><p>Each morning she wrote three lines in the almanac: the wind, the colour of the water, and one thing she had not expected. The third line was the hardest. Some days she sat with the pen above the page until her tea went cold.</p><p>Patience, her uncle had told her when he handed over the keys, is not waiting. It is paying attention while you wait. She had thought it a pretty saying then. Now she knew it was simply a description of the work.</p><p>The lamp was lit at dusk and put out at dawn. Between those hours the lighthouse belonged to the ships, and the ships did not know her name. That suited her. A good light, she thought, should be noticed only when it is missing.</p></section><section><title><p>II. The Storm Book</p></title><p>In the third winter a storm came that the almanac had no words for. The barometer fell so fast that Mara tapped the glass, thinking it stuck. Outside, the gulls had gone quiet and the water had turned the grey of old pewter.</p><p>She trimmed the wick, filled the reservoir and climbed the hundred and twelve steps twice more than she needed to, because climbing was something to do with her hands. At midnight the first wave broke over the gallery rail.</p><p>By morning the shingle had moved. The path to the village was gone, and a new beach lay where the boathouse had been. She opened the almanac and wrote, under the third line, a single word: everything.</p></section><section><title><p>III. Small Repairs</p></title><p>Spring came slowly. The village sent a cart along the new beach with timber and nails, and two boys who wanted to see the lamp. Mara let them polish the brass, which they did with more enthusiasm than care.</p><p>Repairs, she found, were mostly small. A hinge here, a pane of glass there, a stair tread that had worked loose. None of it was dramatic, and all of it mattered. The light did not care how the tower looked; it cared whether the tower stood.</p><p>On the first warm evening she carried a chair onto the gallery and watched the fishing boats come home, one after another, each one turning a little as it found her beam. She did not write anything in the almanac that night. Some days, she decided, the third line could be left blank.</p></section></body></FictionBook>",
